  ]
}


### ======================================================================
### 4. WHO may do this   (owner only)
### ======================================================================

### Z1. Bob (STAFF) tries to change his own hours. EXPECT 403
PUT {{baseUrl}}/staff/2/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "intervals": [ { "dayOfWeek": "SATURDAY", "start": "09:00", "end": "17:00" } ] }

### Z2. Bob reads his own hours. EXPECT 403 (owner only for now; a staff view is a stretch goal)
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/2/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}

### Z3. A USER token (no business selected). EXPECT 403
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/2/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceUserToken}}

### Z4. No token. EXPECT 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/2/working-hours


### ======================================================================
### 5. TENANT ISOLATION   (Alice must never reach tenant 2)
### ======================================================================

### X1. Alice reads Gus's schedule (membership 4, tenant 2). EXPECT 404
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/4/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}

### X2. Alice overwrites Gus's schedule. EXPECT 404 (and Gus's data unchanged, see X5/X6)
PUT {{baseUrl}}/staff/4/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "intervals": [ { "dayOfWeek": "SUNDAY", "start": "00:00", "end": "01:00" } ] }

### X3. Alice reads Bob's membership IN TENANT 2 (membership 3). EXPECT 404
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/3/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}

### X4. An id that exists nowhere. EXPECT 404, the same shape as X1
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/99999/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}

### X5. Gus reads his own schedule. EXPECT 200, 5 intervals Mon-Fri 08:00-16:00 (X2 changed nothing)
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/4/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{gusToken}}

### X6. Gus tries to read ALICE's schedule (membership 1, tenant 1). EXPECT 404
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/1/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{gusToken}}

### X7. Gus sets his own Saturday hours. EXPECT 200, 1 interval
PUT {{baseUrl}}/staff/4/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{gusToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "intervals": [ { "dayOfWeek": "SATURDAY", "start": "09:00", "end": "12:00" } ] }

### X8. Alice's own week is untouched by X7. EXPECT 200, 5 intervals
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff/1/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}


### ======================================================================
### 6. OPTIONAL EXPERIMENT (no fixed expectation: observe and think)
### Jackson can usually also read a number for an enum. What day does 1 mean to it?
### Look at the response and compare with java.time.DayOfWeek.of(1).
### This is why the API contract uses day NAMES, never numbers.
### ======================================================================

### E1. A number instead of a name. Note the status code and which day comes back.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/staff/1/working-hours
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "intervals": [ { "dayOfWeek": 1, "start": "09:00", "end": "10:00" } ] }
